Tinea Infection
Young Adult & Adolecence
Annular scaly plaques, Central clearing, Intense pruritus

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-047
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, generalized pruritus causing extensive skin excoriation and secondary sleep deprivation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage detailed a rapidly spreading, intensely itchy rash with prominent scaling, causing severe sleep disruption and secondary painful skin breakdown due to relentless scratching. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to exclude a severe spreading bacterial cellulitis, and to provide immediate clinical diagnosis and potent topical therapy to break the severe itch-scratch cycle prior to morning clinical availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "These red, scaly rings are spreading all over my body, the itch is driving me insane, and I am bleeding from scratching so much."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a 3-week history of an increasingly pruritic rash. Initially starting as a small red patch on the abdomen, it has rapidly spread to the trunk and proximal extremities. The patient describes multiple annular erythematous plaques with a prominent scaly advancing border. Over time, central clearing has developed within the annular lesions. The primary distress is the intense localized pruritus, which severely disrupts sleep. The patient notes an asymmetrical distribution on the glabrous skin and admits to an exacerbation of symptoms recently due to working in a warm, humid environment. The patient also reports recent contact with a stray kitten.
Functional Status: Independent, but severely fatigued and actively scratching during the consultation.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of secondary bacterial infection due to widespread excoriation; significant sleep deprivation impacting daytime function.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/75 | HR: 85 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient appears exhausted, anxious, and exhibits frequent, aggressive scratching of the torso and arms.
Physical Exam: Dermatological examination reveals multiple 3-8 cm annular erythematous plaques distributed asymmetrically across the torso, back, and bilateral proximal thighs. The lesions exhibit a prominent, raised, scaly advancing border with distinct central clearing. Several areas show linear excoriations with pinpoint dried blood, but there is no spreading cellulitic erythema, warmth, or purulent exudate to suggest secondary bacterial infection. Examination of the intertriginous web spaces (toes) reveals mild maceration and fissuring (tinea pedis). The clinical presentation firmly fulfills the diagnostic tetrad of annular scaly plaques, central clearing, and intense pruritus.
Environmental Observations: Patient's room is heavily heated and poorly ventilated, actively promoting a humid environment conducive to fungal proliferation.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has been mistakenly applying a high-potency topical corticosteroid borrowed from a friend, which has exacerbated the fungal spread (tinea incognito).

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Extensive Tinea Corporis (Ringworm) with concurrent Tinea Pedis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Pityriasis Rosea - Ruled out/unlikely due to the asymmetrical distribution, the extreme intensity of the pruritus, and the lack of a classic "herald patch" or Christmas-tree distribution on the back.

Nummular Eczema - Ruled out/unlikely due to the prominent central clearing and raised scaly borders, whereas nummular eczema presents as uniform, intensely weeping, coin-shaped coin plaques without central clearing.

Secondary Bacterial Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the strict absence of fever, localized heat, severe swelling, and the clearly defined, slow-spreading annular borders.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, intolerable exacerbation of pruritus driven by a widespread dermatophyte infection, worsened by the inappropriate use of topical corticosteroids. The sheer intensity of the itch, causing severe sleep deprivation and extensive skin excoriation, necessitated an immediate after-hours home visit. Delaying assessment would have resulted in continued severe distress, further inappropriate steroid application, and a high risk of true secondary bacterial impetiginization. Immediate clinical evaluation safely excluded acute bacterial cellulitis, identified the fungal etiology, and allowed for the immediate cessation of the exacerbating steroid and initiation of targeted antifungal and symptomatic therapies to secure sleep.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Ordered the immediate cessation of the borrowed topical corticosteroid. Prescribed Terbinafine 1% cream to be applied strictly to the lesions and 2 cm beyond the advancing borders twice daily for 2-4 weeks. Administered Promethazine 25mg PO stat to provide immediate sedation and break the acute pruritic cycle tonight. Recommended skin scrapings for KOH preparation via the regular GP to formally confirm the presence of branching septate hyphae if the rash proves refractory.
Positive Behavioral Change: To eliminate the humid environment that the fungus requires to survive, thoroughly dry all skin folds and web spaces completely with a dedicated, separate towel after showering, actively removing the moisture that fuels fungal growth. To prevent reinfection and spread, wash all bed linens and clothing immediately in a hot water cycle (above 60 degrees Celsius), physically destroying the fungal spores trapped in the fabric.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks to monitor clinical resolution; if the extensive spread does not halt, oral antifungal therapy (e.g., oral Terbinafine) may be required.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department is required if the scratched skin suddenly becomes intensely red, swollen, hot to the touch, and rapidly spreads, or if a high fever and rigors develop.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinically highly consistent with a fungal infection (ringworm); formal skin scrapings can confirm the exact fungal species if it does not clear.
Clinical Course: The severe itch will be heavily reduced by the antihistamine tonight, but the fungal rings will take 2 to 4 weeks of continuous cream application to fully disappear.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention immediately if the skin around the scratches turns bright red, becomes hot and swollen, or if you develop a high fever and uncontrollable shivering.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your skin is infected with a common, superficial fungus. The steroid cream you were using essentially turned off your local immune system, allowing the fungus to feed and spread rapidly. The new antifungal cream directly kills the fungal cells, while the sedating tablet will force you to sleep and stop the scratching tonight.
Actionable Steps: Stop using the steroid cream immediately. Apply the antifungal cream past the red border of the rings to catch the spreading edge. Wash your hands thoroughly after applying the cream to avoid spreading it to your face or eyes.

